WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:02.890
Happy New Year.

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:07.399
I have a New Year's video prepared,
but before I start that video, I wanted

00:00:07.399 --> 00:00:12.649
to jump in with a story and something
that's very important for 2024.

00:00:12.729 --> 00:00:16.849
I was at our golf club and the wife
of one of our pros says to me, I

00:00:16.849 --> 00:00:18.799
tried learning French on your system.

00:00:18.889 --> 00:00:22.250
I understand, but I can't
speak, so I gave up.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.820
I thought that was very, very important.

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:25.150
Most people give up.

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:29.000
The biggest problem language learners
have is they don't stay with it.

00:00:29.445 --> 00:00:33.475
Another problem is, some people
convince themselves that they understand

00:00:33.475 --> 00:00:36.015
well, but they just can't speak well.

00:00:36.195 --> 00:00:39.524
First of all, she probably
doesn't understand that well.

00:00:39.675 --> 00:00:42.955
If you have very good comprehension,
like in many different

00:00:42.955 --> 00:00:46.794
situations, movies, three or four
people talking to each other.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.010
If you don't understand those situations,
you don't have good comprehension.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:51.910
So you have to have good comprehension.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:56.040
But you also can't give up because
language learning actually is not easy.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.870
I sometimes liken it to a jigsaw puzzle.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.690
But imagine a jigsaw puzzle where
you don't know what the picture

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:05.039
looks like and all the little pieces.

00:01:05.254 --> 00:01:09.315
Words, phrases that you pick up and
put into the picture, they disappear

00:01:09.315 --> 00:01:11.035
on you because you forget them.

00:01:11.295 --> 00:01:13.245
And so, there's this
continuing frustration.

00:01:13.485 --> 00:01:18.675
The only way you gradually develop a
sense of the language is by continuing.

00:01:18.755 --> 00:01:21.634
You're listening and reading and speaking.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:28.520
And the good news for 2024 is it has
never been easier to acquire the language

00:01:28.579 --> 00:01:32.100
through listening and reading because
all the other activities, reviewing

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.759
words, which I do, looking at grammar,
which I do, speaking to people, which

00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:42.625
I do, it all hinges on having So, yeah,
Content of interest and never has it

00:01:42.625 --> 00:01:47.305
been easier to find content of interest
and to bring it in as a video, as an

00:01:47.305 --> 00:01:52.455
audio file, as a text file into LingQ or
some other system and use it to learn.

00:01:52.595 --> 00:01:56.514
By the way, make sure you take
advantage of LingQ's end of the

00:01:56.514 --> 00:01:59.235
year or new year special deals.

00:01:59.355 --> 00:02:03.075
Avail yourself of the access
code and get studying.

00:02:03.085 --> 00:02:07.130
Now, without further ado, I will
give you The New Year's video

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:08.660
that I produced a little earlier.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:15.094
So New Year's resolutions, what
do I want to do in 2024 here?

00:02:15.094 --> 00:02:20.109
I'll be mostly 78 for
most of the upcoming year.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.630
So insofar as my channel is
concerned, I want to continue

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:25.250
talking about language learning.

00:02:25.299 --> 00:02:27.600
I hope to do maybe.

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:29.670
One foreign language, i.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:29.829
e.

00:02:29.829 --> 00:02:33.920
non English video every
month, maybe every six weeks.

00:02:33.989 --> 00:02:36.559
I want to continue talking about
language learning, of course, but

00:02:36.559 --> 00:02:41.329
I maybe want to expand it into the
subject of learning in general, because

00:02:41.339 --> 00:02:42.679
these are things that interest me.

00:02:42.689 --> 00:02:47.039
So I want to take my interests and
share them with you here at my channel.

00:02:47.059 --> 00:02:51.039
So in talking about the subject of
learning and how the brain learns

00:02:51.039 --> 00:02:54.489
more broadly, I will be commenting
on some of the things that.

00:02:54.769 --> 00:02:59.140
Other people in, you know, on the
internet through YouTube, influencers,

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:02.510
what they are saying and give you
my sort of reaction to some of the

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:04.740
things that I find on the internet.

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:09.600
Insofar as language is concerned, I want
to continue with my Arabic and Persian

00:03:09.729 --> 00:03:13.720
because I'm certainly not satisfied
with where I am in those languages.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.870
In fact, I should promise that I
will do a video both in Persian

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.460
and in Arabic in the upcoming year.

00:03:19.644 --> 00:03:23.695
Of course, my involvement with these
languages is also a part of my interest

00:03:23.695 --> 00:03:28.655
in those histories and those cultures,
because when I learn a language, I

00:03:28.915 --> 00:03:32.944
start to sort of get a taste of the
language and of the culture, but

00:03:32.944 --> 00:03:36.665
then that drives me to do a lot of
reading, uh, about those people.

00:03:36.705 --> 00:03:39.749
So right now, for example, I'm,
I'm listening to an audio book.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:45.930
On the Arabs, but I also bought the
book and it's by Tim McIntosh Smith.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:47.110
It's fascinating.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:50.910
It makes me realize that, you
know, the Arabs, they didn't just

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:52.810
arrive on the scene with Mohammed.

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:57.719
The Arabs as a people from South Arabia
through to the fertile Crescent have

00:03:57.719 --> 00:04:01.750
been interacting with each other and with
neighboring peoples for a long, long time.

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.230
And it's very useful to
get a sense of all of that.

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.890
It puts my Arabic learning.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:11.100
In a context, and of course the Arabic
learning is not just standard Arabic

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:15.510
where I began, but it's also more
now Levantine Arabic with, I've had,

00:04:15.739 --> 00:04:18.230
had some forays into Egyptian Arabic.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:22.690
And of course this helps because you don't
know if you come across an Arabic speaker,

00:04:22.719 --> 00:04:24.539
which form of Arabic they're going to use.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:26.909
So I will continue my explorations there.

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:28.450
My Persian learning has.

00:04:28.784 --> 00:04:32.875
Progress much more quickly than my
Arabic because it's one language.

00:04:32.924 --> 00:04:37.414
And I have done a lot of reading, you
know, history of Iran here, a history

00:04:37.414 --> 00:04:39.625
of the Tajiks and what else have we got?

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:41.505
The Persianate world.

00:04:41.554 --> 00:04:44.884
And these are just some, you
know, lost enlightenment.

00:04:45.625 --> 00:04:49.445
You know, which again, talks about
Central Asia, which is largely Persian

00:04:49.445 --> 00:04:51.604
speaking, but also Turkish speaking.

00:04:51.604 --> 00:04:55.844
And of course I did read a book
on Salim the Magnificent, Turkish.

00:04:55.844 --> 00:05:02.075
And of course the Greeks have also been
on the edge of certainly Central Asia have

00:05:02.075 --> 00:05:04.134
interacted with Arabic speaking people.

00:05:04.134 --> 00:05:05.984
So it's all part of that discovery.

00:05:06.194 --> 00:05:09.194
So I will definitely continue
working on those languages.

00:05:09.224 --> 00:05:10.134
I may.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:15.570
Go after Turkish, but if we get Hindi
at LingQ, which I'm hoping we do, then

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:20.600
I want to start exploring India and
learning Hindi, it's not a necessity.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:23.710
I understand that if you go to India,
a lot of people speak English, but

00:05:23.779 --> 00:05:27.740
I just feel that when I get into a
language, it just introduces me to the

00:05:27.769 --> 00:05:29.149
culture, the history in a different way.

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:30.199
I get very interested.

00:05:30.199 --> 00:05:32.170
I read it more and more on the subject.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.240
So I think I may start in
Hindi this coming year.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:37.059
We will see.

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:39.220
In any case, I will be dealing with.

00:05:39.465 --> 00:05:45.415
Languages that I'm at, uh, call it, uh,
I might be B1 in Persian, A2 in Arabic.

00:05:45.495 --> 00:05:47.725
So those are languages that
I'm in the middle of learning.

00:05:47.905 --> 00:05:52.174
But if I take Hindi on, then
it'll be a brand new language and

00:05:52.174 --> 00:05:53.444
of course, a new writing system.

00:05:53.814 --> 00:05:57.965
So, you know, if I think of those
experiences and what advice I might

00:05:57.965 --> 00:06:02.235
have for people Who are either
starting from scratch or, you know,

00:06:02.235 --> 00:06:03.615
wanting to improve in a language.

00:06:03.665 --> 00:06:06.785
My advice usually is you obviously
want to follow your interests.

00:06:06.795 --> 00:06:10.445
So if you're interested in, in grammar,
you're going to look into the grammar.

00:06:10.454 --> 00:06:14.225
Whenever you are interested, whenever
you're curious, following your curiosity

00:06:14.225 --> 00:06:16.084
is always the right thing to do.

00:06:16.225 --> 00:06:20.339
But there are moments when you feel
you're not getting anywhere or with a

00:06:20.339 --> 00:06:23.927
brand new language, particularly in a
brand new writing system, you wonder

00:06:23.927 --> 00:06:26.319
if you are ever going to get there.

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:29.740
If you will ever start to make
sense of this language, and I have

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:31.820
had that feeling so many times.

00:06:32.255 --> 00:06:35.295
Particularly most recently with
Arabic and Persian, trying to

00:06:35.295 --> 00:06:38.715
learn the script, hearing all these
sounds that are totally new to me.

00:06:38.935 --> 00:06:43.874
And yet, while I'm not as good as I
would like to be, I have come a long way.

00:06:44.124 --> 00:06:49.644
So I guess what I would suggest to people
as you go into the new year and make your

00:06:49.655 --> 00:06:54.815
new year's resolutions, remember that the
heavy lifting is the amount of time that

00:06:54.815 --> 00:06:56.885
you have to spend listening and reading.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:01.310
That's where you will get the biggest
return on your investment of time.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:03.250
Don't worry about things you forget.

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:05.190
Don't worry about words you forget.

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:08.369
Don't worry about grammar rules
that you forget or that you can't

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:10.069
follow when you're trying to speak.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:10.990
None of that matters.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:15.890
One thing matters, staying active,
finding ways to enjoy the process,

00:07:15.919 --> 00:07:19.400
finding the process of learning a
language relevant and important to you.

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.470
If you can continue to do
that, you will succeed.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.580
So I wish everyone success.

00:07:24.885 --> 00:07:28.945
In the new year, in whatever they
choose to do, I didn't talk about

00:07:28.975 --> 00:07:32.895
what my personal goals for the
new year are because they are

00:07:32.905 --> 00:07:37.114
personal, but they relate to family,
friends, enjoying all the wonderful

00:07:37.135 --> 00:07:38.984
things that we enjoy in this life.

00:07:39.035 --> 00:07:41.244
So a happy new year to everyone.

00:07:41.854 --> 00:07:42.314
Bye for now.

